---

<MainLayout
  title="TorqTrace Case Study | Ashleigh Magloire"
  description="A WordPress frontend dashboard case study covering TorqTrace, an automotive diagnostic interface concept built with WordPress, PHP and responsive CSS."
>
  <Navbar client:load />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#F7F6F1] text-[#1E2823]">
    <div class="w-full px-6 pb-20 pt-28 md:px-10 md:pt-32">
      <CaseStudyHero
        eyebrow="Case Study · WordPress · Automotive Dashboard"
        title="TorqTrace"
        summary="A WordPress frontend dashboard concept for automotive diagnostics, featuring vehicle health metrics, fault-code monitoring, inspection queues and responsive interface design."
        tags={torqtraceTags}
        githubUrl="https://github.com/Iris408/torqtrace"
        image="/screenshots/torqtrace/torqtrace-desktop-hero.png"
        imageAlt="TorqTrace automotive diagnostic dashboard"
      />

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Overview"
        title="A diagnostic dashboard concept rebuilt as a WordPress theme"
      >
        <p>
          TorqTrace is a frontend dashboard concept for vehicle diagnostics. It
          presents technician-focused information such as fleet health, active
          alerts, recent fault codes, inspection priorities and maintenance
          signals.
        </p>

        <p>
          The project originally existed as a smaller React and TypeScript
          frontend demo. I rebuilt it as a custom WordPress theme to practise
          theme structure, responsive CSS, dashboard composition and
          screenshot-ready visual polish.
        </p>
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Problem"
        title="The original concept needed a stronger portfolio presentation"
      >
        <p>
          The earlier diagnostic UI demonstrated useful frontend ideas, but it
          felt more like a technical exercise than a finished project. I wanted
          the remodel to feel more intentional, visually stronger and easier to
          present in screenshots.
        </p>

        <p>
          TorqTrace therefore needed a cleaner product identity, a more polished
          dashboard layout and a responsive structure that worked properly
          across desktop, tablet and mobile views.
        </p>
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Solution"
        title="A focused WordPress dashboard concept"
      >
        <p>
          I kept the project deliberately scoped as a static WordPress frontend
          concept. Instead of adding backend logic, I focused on the dashboard
          layout, theme files, responsive styling and the automotive diagnostic
          product experience.
        </p>

        <FeatureGrid features={torqtraceFeatures} />
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Product Experience"
        title="Desktop, tablet and mobile dashboard views"
      >
        <ImageGallery images={torqtraceGallery} />
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Architecture"
        title="How TorqTrace is structured"
      >
        <p>
          TorqTrace is organised as a custom WordPress theme. The main dashboard
          is rendered through front-page.php, with shared structure handled by
          the header, footer and fallback template files.
        </p>

        <ArchitectureDiagram items={torqtraceArchitecture} />

        <TechStack groups={torqtraceTechStack} />
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Development Decisions"
        title="Challenges I worked through"
      >
        <div class="grid gap-6">
          {torqtraceChallenges.map((item) => (
            <ChallengeCard
              title={item.title}
              challenge={item.challenge}
              decision={item.decision}
              outcome={item.outcome}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Learning"
        title="What this project taught me"
      >
        <p>
          TorqTrace helped me practise WordPress theme development while
          keeping the project connected to automotive workflows and dashboard
          interface design.
        </p>

        <LearningList items={torqtraceLearnings} />
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Development"
        title="How TorqTrace evolved"
      >
        <Timeline items={torqtraceTimeline} />
      </CaseStudySection>

      <CaseStudySection
        eyebrow="Next Steps"
        title="Possible future improvements"
      >
        <FeatureGrid features={torqtraceFutureImprovements} />
      </CaseStudySection>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</MainLayout>